\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Comparison of specification inference approaches. Precision, recall, and coverage figures for JML-Inferrer, Jdoctor, and the LLM-based baseline are computed on the same 312-method corpus and are directly comparable. PreInfer~\cite  {preinfer2017} targets a different language family (C\#) and could not be applied to the Java corpus; figures are not reported (N/R) because we did not reproduce the tool against an equivalent C\# corpus within the scope of this study. Daikon~\cite  {ernst2007daikon} requires an execution infrastructure (test suites or instrumented runs), which is incompatible with the static-only methodology of this article; ``varies'' indicates that figures published in the original article depend strongly on the choice of test suite.}}{6}{table.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:approach-comparison-supp}{{3}{6}{Comparison of specification inference approaches. Precision, recall, and coverage figures for JML-Inferrer, Jdoctor, and the LLM-based baseline are computed on the same 312-method corpus and are directly comparable. PreInfer~\cite {preinfer2017} targets a different language family (C\#) and could not be applied to the Java corpus; figures are not reported (N/R) because we did not reproduce the tool against an equivalent C\# corpus within the scope of this study. Daikon~\cite {ernst2007daikon} requires an execution infrastructure (test suites or instrumented runs), which is incompatible with the static-only methodology of this article; ``varies'' indicates that figures published in the original article depend strongly on the choice of test suite}{table.3}{}}
